      "Before a cell divides it copies its DNA. The enzyme helicase unwinds the helix, and each strand serves as a template for a new one, built by DNA polymerase according to base pairing. Replication is semiconservative: each new double helix keeps one original strand and gains one new strand, as Matthew Meselson and Franklin Stahl showed in 1958.",
      "A gene is a stretch of DNA that codes for a product, usually a protein. In transcription, RNA polymerase copies a gene into messenger RNA (mRNA), which uses the base uracil (U) in place of thymine. In eukaryotes transcription happens in the nucleus, and the mRNA is processed before it moves out to the cytoplasm.",
      "In translation, ribosomes read the mRNA three bases at a time. Each three-base codon specifies an amino acid or a stop signal, and transfer RNA (tRNA) molecules with matching anticodons deliver the amino acids, which are joined into a polypeptide chain. The genetic code is nearly universal and is redundant: most amino acids are coded by more than one codon, so a mutation that changes one base may change the protein or may have no effect at all."
