    "instructions": "The examiner will talk with you about how living things capture and use energy: ATP, photosynthesis, aerobic respiration and fermentation. Answer in your own words and explain why, not only what.",
    "passage": [
      "Cells need a steady supply of usable energy, which they hold in the molecule ATP (adenosine triphosphate). Photosynthesis captures light energy and stores it in sugar; cellular respiration releases the energy in sugar and uses it to make ATP. Plants and algae do both; animals and fungi only respire.",
      "Photosynthesis takes place in chloroplasts. Its overall equation is 6CO2 + 6H2O + light energy -> C6H12O6 + 6O2. In the light-dependent reactions, in the thylakoid membranes, chlorophyll absorbs light and water is split, releasing oxygen and producing ATP and NADPH. In the Calvin cycle, in the stroma, that ATP and NADPH are used to fix carbon dioxide into sugar.",
      "Aerobic respiration is roughly the reverse overall: C6H12O6 + 6O2 -> 6CO2 + 6H2O + energy. Glycolysis splits glucose into pyruvate in the cytoplasm; the Krebs (citric acid) cycle and the electron transport chain in the mitochondria complete the breakdown, with oxygen as the final electron acceptor. One glucose molecule yields about 30 to 32 ATP.",
      "Without oxygen, many cells rely on fermentation, which follows glycolysis and yields only 2 ATP per glucose. Human muscle cells make lactate; yeast makes ethanol and carbon dioxide. Plants respire all the time, day and night, while they photosynthesize only in the light."
    ],
