  {
    "title": "Mitosis and Meiosis",
    "category": "life-sciences",
    "subject": "Biology",
    "topic": "Cell division",
    "instructions": "The examiner will talk with you about cell division: chromosomes, mitosis, meiosis and how meiosis makes every gamete different. Answer in your own words and explain why, not only what.",
    "passage": [
      "Cells reproduce by dividing. Before division, the cell copies its DNA during interphase, so each chromosome then consists of two identical sister chromatids joined at a centromere. Human body cells are diploid: they have 46 chromosomes in 23 homologous pairs, one chromosome of each pair from each parent.",
      "Mitosis divides the nucleus to make two genetically identical diploid cells. In prophase the chromosomes condense; in metaphase they line up across the middle of the cell; in anaphase the sister chromatids are pulled to opposite poles; in telophase new nuclei form. Cytokinesis then divides the cytoplasm. Mitosis serves growth, repair and asexual reproduction.",
      "Meiosis makes gametes. It has two divisions and produces four haploid cells, each with one chromosome from every homologous pair: 23 in humans. In meiosis I the homologous chromosomes pair up and separate; in meiosis II the sister chromatids separate. Fertilization joins two haploid gametes and restores the diploid number.",
      "Meiosis makes each gamete genetically different. During prophase I, homologous chromosomes exchange segments in crossing over, and the way each pair lines up at metaphase I is random, so in humans independent assortment alone allows over 8 million (2 to the 23rd power) combinations. Errors in separation, called nondisjunction, can leave a gamete with an extra or a missing chromosome, as in Down syndrome (trisomy 21)."
