    "passage": [
      "Homeostasis is the maintenance of a stable internal environment despite changes outside. The human body keeps its core temperature near 37 degrees Celsius, its blood glucose within a narrow range, and the water and salt levels of its blood steady, because enzymes and cells work properly only within limits.",
      "Most homeostasis uses negative feedback. A receptor detects a change, a control center such as the brain compares it with a set point, and an effector responds to reverse the change. When body temperature rises, the hypothalamus triggers sweating and widens blood vessels in the skin so more heat is lost; when it falls, shivering produces heat and skin blood vessels narrow.",
      "Blood glucose is regulated by two hormones from the pancreas. After a meal, rising glucose triggers the release of insulin, which makes cells take up glucose and the liver store it as glycogen. When glucose falls, glucagon makes the liver break glycogen down and release glucose. In type 1 diabetes the pancreas makes little or no insulin; in type 2 diabetes the body's cells respond poorly to it.",
      "Positive feedback, by contrast, amplifies a change instead of reversing it, driving a process to completion. During childbirth, the hormone oxytocin strengthens contractions, which press the baby against the cervix, which causes more oxytocin to be released, until birth ends the loop. Blood clotting is another example."
    ],
