    "title": "Classification and the Tree of Life",
    "category": "life-sciences",
    "subject": "Biology",
    "topic": "Systematics",
    "instructions": "The examiner will talk with you about how living things are named and classified: species names, the ranks, the three domains and how to read a phylogenetic tree. Answer in your own words and explain why, not only what.",
    "passage": [
      "Biologists name and classify the millions of species on Earth so they can be studied and compared. Carl Linnaeus introduced binomial nomenclature in the 18th century: each species has a two-part Latin name made of its genus and a specific epithet, such as Homo sapiens, written in italics with the genus capitalized.",
      "Species are grouped into a hierarchy of ranks: domain, kingdom, phylum, class, order, family, genus and species. Organisms that share more ranks are more closely related. Following the work of Carl Woese in 1977, life is divided into three domains: Bacteria, Archaea and Eukarya. Archaea are prokaryotes like bacteria, yet in some ways, such as how they copy and read their DNA, they are closer to eukaryotes.",
      "Modern classification aims to reflect evolutionary history. A phylogenetic tree shows how species are related through common ancestors: each branch point, or node, represents a common ancestor, and a clade is an ancestor together with all of its descendants. Species that share a more recent common ancestor are more closely related, however alike or unlike they look.",
      "Trees are built from shared derived characteristics, traits that arose in a common ancestor and were passed to its descendants, and increasingly from DNA sequences. Similarity can mislead: the wings of birds and bats evolved separately, as analogous structures produced by convergent evolution, so they do not show a close relationship. Humans did not evolve from chimpanzees; the two share a common ancestor that lived roughly 6 to 7 million years ago."
    ],
